1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,590
Today we're going to look at an important area of science, namely nanotechnology.

2
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:08,977
So what is it?

3
00:00:08,977 --> 00:00:10,425
Nano means tiny,

4
00:00:10,425 --> 00:00:17,000
so it's science and engineering on the scale of atoms and molecules.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,290
The idea is that by controlling and rearranging atoms, you can literally create anything.

6
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:30,847
However, as well see, the science of the small has some big implications affecting

7
00:00:30,847 --> 00:00:32,720
us in many ways.

8
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:38,780
There's no doubt that nanotechnology promises so much for civilisation.

9
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:43,420
However, all new technologies have their teething problems.

10
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:50,000
And with nanotechnology, society often gets the wrong idea about its capabilities.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Numerous science-fiction books and movies have raised people's fears about nanotechnology - with scenarios

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,538
such as inserting little nano-robots into your body that monitor everything you do without

13
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:06,154
you realising it,

14
00:01:06,154 --> 00:01:11,000
or self-replicating nano-robots that eventually take over the world.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,210
So how do we safeguard such a potentially powerful technology?

16
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:24,951
Some scientists recommend that nano-particles be treated as new chemicals with separate safety tests

17
00:01:24,951 --> 00:01:26,610
and clear labelling.

18
00:01:26,610 --> 00:01:32,287
They believe that greater care should also be taken with nano-particles in laboratories

19
00:01:32,287 --> 00:01:33,160
and factories.

20
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:38,857
Others have called for a withdrawal of new nano products such as cosmetics

21
00:01:38,857 --> 00:01:43,240
and a temporary halt to many kinds of nanotech research.

22
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:49,264
But as far as l'm concerned there's a need to plough ahead with the discoveries

23
00:01:49,264 --> 00:01:50,870
and applications of nanotechnology.

24
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:56,882
I really believe that most scientists would welcome a way to guard against unethical

25
00:01:56,882 --> 00:01:58,600
uses of such technology.

26
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:04,770
We can't go around thinking that all innovation is bad, all advancement is bad.

27
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:10,055
As with the debate about any new technology, it is how you use it

28
00:02:10,055 --> 00:02:10,810
that's important.

29
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:19,050
So let's look at some of its possible uses.

30
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:25,777
Thanks to nanotechnology, there could be a major breakthrough in the field of transportation

31
00:02:25,777 --> 00:02:29,140
with the production of more durable metals.

32
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:35,633
These could be virtually unbreakable, lighter and much more pliable leading to planes that

33
00:02:35,633 --> 00:02:38,880
are 50 times lighter than at present.

34
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:45,170
Those same improved capabilities will dramatically reduce the cost of travelling into space making

35
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:51,460
it more accessible to ordinary people and opening up a totally new holiday destination.

36
00:02:51,460 --> 00:02:58,320
In terms of technology, the computer industry will be able to shrink computer parts

37
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,280
down to minute sizes.

38
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:07,342
We need nanotechnology in order to create a new generation of computers that will work even faster

39
00:03:07,342 --> 00:03:13,158
and will have a million times more memory but will be about the size

40
00:03:13,158 --> 00:03:14,820
of a sugar cube.

41
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:20,100
Nanotechnology could also revolutionise the way that we generate power.

42
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:25,753
The cost of solar cells will be drastically reduced so harnessing this energy will

43
00:03:25,753 --> 00:03:28,580
be far more economical than at present.

44
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:36,453
But nanotechnology has much wider applications than this and could have an enormous impact

45
00:03:36,453 --> 00:03:38,140
on our environment.

46
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:45,207
For instance, tiny airborne nano-robots could be programmed to actually rebuild the ozone layer,

47
00:03:45,207 --> 00:03:50,760
which could lessen the impact of global warming on our planet.

48
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:54,270
That's a pretty amazing thought, isn't it?

49
00:03:54,270 --> 00:04:00,566
On a more local scale, this new technology could help with the clean-up of

50
00:04:00,566 --> 00:04:05,063
environmental disasters as nanotechnology will allow us to remove oil

51
00:04:05,063 --> 00:04:09,110
and other contaminants from the water far more effectively.

52
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:14,788
And, if nanotechnology progresses as expected - as a sort of building block set

53
00:04:14,788 --> 00:04:20,903
of about 90 atoms -then you could build anything you wanted from the bottom

54
00:04:20,903 --> 00:04:21,340
up.

55
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:26,779
In terms of production, this means that you only use what you need and

56
00:04:26,779 --> 00:04:29,110
so there wouldn't be any waste.

57
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:34,775
The notion that you could create anything at all has major implications for our

58
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:35,180
health.

59
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:39,630
It means that we'll eventually be able to replicate anything.

60
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:43,330
This would have a phenomenal effect on our society.

61
00:04:43,330 --> 00:04:48,449
In time it could even lead to the eradication of famine through the introduction

62
00:04:48,449 --> 00:04:51,740
of machines that produce food to feed the hungry.

63
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:58,780
But it's in the area of medicine that nanotechnology may have its biggest impact.

64
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:05,478
How we detect disease will change as tiny biosensors are developed to analyse tests

65
00:05:05,478 --> 00:05:07,870
in minutes rather than days.

66
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:15,729
There's even speculation nano-robots could be used to slow the ageing process, lengthening life

67
00:05:15,729 --> 00:05:16,290
expectancy.

68
00:05:16,290 --> 00:05:21,430
As you can see, I'm very excited by the implications that could be available

69
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:24,000
to us in the next few decades.

70
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,420
Just how long it'll take, I honestly don't know.
